es"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_053","cnm_054","cnm_055"],"id":"radial-point-distance-bound","label":"Compare with the radial point","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radial-point-distance-bound":["math:nearest-point-on-circle","cnm_111"]},"nodeIds":["math:nearest-point-on-circle","cnm_111","cnm_250","cnm_053","cnm_054","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native mathematical/wording adjudication of the exact displayed task. Proposal for root inspection; raw targeted review is not blind diverse corroboration.","derivedAnswer":"85 degrees for opposite ray pairs;275/3 degrees for another configuration satisfying the displayed givens.","reason":"Saying that full linesAB andCD intersect atO does not state thatO lies betweenA,B and also betweenC,D. With both pairs opposite, the named angles are vertical and x=25 gives85 degrees. Keep A=(1,0),B=(-1,0),O=(0,0), but put C on a ray at275/3 degrees andD=2C on the same ray. Then angleAOC=275/3 and angleBOD=265/3, satisfying the two given expressions with x=80/3. Both full lines still intersect atO. No displayed figure supplies the missing point order. The topic heading identifies intended vertical-angle content but does not impose the missing ray incidence.","recordedAnswer":"85","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["confirmed_answer_ambiguity","missing_point_order_or_ray_incidence"],"questionId":"cnm_063_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_029"],"id":"analyze-ray-placements","label":"Consider the possible ray placements","methodDirectNodeIds":{"analyze-ray-placements":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. (1) or (2) only","reason":"The vectors OP and QR are equal, as are OQ and PR. Noncollinear P and Q therefore give a parallelogram; collinear position vectors give a straight-line figure.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Uses the exclusive definition of trapezoid: exactly one pair of parallel sides. An inclusive definition would change the choice.","The answer is D when a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel sides. With the inclusive definition, parallelograms are also trapezoids and E would apply."],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_067","cnm_053"],"id":"coordinate-translations","label":"Compare opposite sides using translations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-translations":["cnm_086","cnm_072","cnm_184","cnm_p105"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_086","cnm_072","cnm_184","cnm_p105","cnm_082","cnm_067","cnm_053"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, PQ always equals the length of an external common tangent","reason":"At P and Q, the rays toward the two centers bisect supplementary tangent angles, so ∠OPO'=∠OQO'=90°. Thus P and Q lie on the circle of diameter d=OO'. Its center is |R-r|/2 from PQ, giving PQ=√(d²-(R-r)²), which is exactly the external contact-to-contact tangent length.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_131","cnm_250","cnm_053"],"id":"solution:equal-tangent-segments:69df0021dce7","label":"Equal tangent segments and symmetry","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangent-segments":["cnm_263","cnm_132","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_132","cnm_055","cnm_262","cnm_131","cnm_250","cnm_053"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (5/2)","reason":"Reflect B across AN to B′. Because AN bisects ∠BAC, B′ lies on AC with AB′ = 14, so B′C = 19 - 14 = 5. Perpendicularity makes N the midpoint of BB′. Since M is the midpoint of BC, MN = B′C/2 = 5/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_064","cnm_131"],"id":"solution:reflection-midsegment:e92efd014e2b","label":"Reflect across the angle bisector","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reflection-midsegment":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_132","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_132","cnm_185","cnm_053","cnm_064","cnm_131"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 90 degrees","reason":"Put alpha=angle ACD=angle BAE. The equilateral triangle gives angle ACE=60+alpha and angle AEC=60, so angle CAE=60-alpha and angle BAC=60. Writing AC=s and AB=2s, the cosine rule gives BC^2=4s^2+s^2-2(2s)(s)(1/2)=3s^2. Hence AB^2=AC^2+BC^2, so angle ACB=90 degrees.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_058","cnm_110"],"id":"angle-chase-and-midpoint","label":"Use the equilateral triangle, then a midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"angle-chase-and-midpoint":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_137","cnm_053","cnm_058","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 5","reason":"Extend BP beyond P to Q with PQ=3. Then DB=1 and DQ=5. Isosceles triangle APQ gives ∠AQD=∠BCD, and vertical angles give triangle DAQ similar to triangle DBC. Thus AD·CD=DB·DQ=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_063","cnm_117","cnm_268"],"id":"solution:extension-and-similarity:653647296e1f","label":"Extend a segment to create similar triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extension-and-similarity":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_063","cnm_117","cnm_268"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"],"id":"auxiliary-circle","label":"Recognize an intersecting-chord product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-circle":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:406ad8959299fa57eb245ecf"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. Same area, but the perimeter of I is smaller.","reason":"Taking adjacent grid spacing as 1, I has area 1 and II consists of two triangles of area 1/2 each. Their perimeters are 2+2√2 and 1+2√2+√5, respectively. The second exceeds the first by √5-1>0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2000_AMC_8_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p147","cnm_p148","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_053"],"id":"equal-areas-matching-sides","label":"Split the areas and match boundary lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-areas-matching-sides":["cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_065"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_065","cnm_p147","cnm_p148","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_053"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_053"],"label":"直线、射线、线段","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能区分三者并正确表示","能画出对应图形"],"description":"认识直线、射线、线段的特征、表示方法及相互区别。","id":"cnm_053","label":"直线、射线、线段","original":{"chapter":"七上 第六章 几何图形初步 6.2.1","description":"认识直线、射线、线段的特征、表示方法及相互区别。","domain":"图形与几何","external":false,"grade":7,"id":"cnm_053","mastery_criteria":["能区分三者并正确表示","能画出对应图形"],"name":"直线、射线、线段","semester":"上","standards":[{"confidence":"exact","id":"cms-2022:4.GG.01"}],"type":"conceptual"},"placement":{"chapter":"七上 第六章 几何图形初步 6.2.1","domain":"图形与几何","grade":7,"semester":"上"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"cnm_053","status":"mapped","total":31}
